(Efficacy Comparison of Regional Anesthesia Techniques Following Cesarean Delivery) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Abstract) Tj
T*
(Sixty postpartum women undergoing cesarean delivery were randomized to receive either an) Tj
T*
(erector spinae plane block \(n = 30\) or a transversus abdominis plane block \(n = 30\) for) Tj
T*
(postoperative analgesia. Pain severity, analgesia duration, and satisfaction were compared) Tj
T*
(between groups. The erector spinae plane block provided analgesia for a mean of 16 hours) Tj
T*
(compared to 14 hours with the transversus abdominis plane block \(not statistically) Tj
T*
(significant, p = 0.32\). Pain scores one hour postoperatively were lower in the erector) Tj
T*
(spinae plane group \(mean 2.1 vs 3.2 on 10-point scale, p = 0.03\). However, no significant) Tj
T*
(differences were observed at subsequent timepoints \(4, 8, 12, 24 hours\). Requirement for) Tj
T*
(rescue analgesia was lower in the erector spinae plane group \(mean 0 vs 1 additional dose,) Tj
T*
(p = 0.045\). Patient satisfaction with pain management was high in both groups \(mean) Tj
T*
(satisfaction 8.4/10 both groups\). Postpartum depression screening scores at 4-6 weeks) Tj
T*
(postpartum were similar between groups \(no significant difference\). Both regional) Tj
T*
(anesthesia techniques provided effective postoperative pain control; the erector spinae) Tj
T*
(plane block showed marginal advantage in early pain scores and reduced additional) Tj
T*
(analgesia requirement, though clinical significance of these differences is limited.) Tj
